        "# Exercise\n",
        "\n",
        "## Exercise 1.\n",
        "The goal of this exercise is to construct a clean dataset for cross-sectional stock return prediction. We keep observations from January 2000 onward, select a limited set of influential predictors, document missing-value handling, and prepare a feature matrix with characteristic - macro interactions and industry dummies."
      ],
      "id": "8204368e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import tidyfinance as tf\n",
        "from plotnine import *\n",
        "from mizani.formatters import percent_format"
      ],
      "id": "a3d19770",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 1a: Variable selection and sample restriction\n",
        "First we read in the monthly stock characteristics data and restricting the sample to observations dated on or after January 1, 2000."
      ],
      "id": "fe8208b5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "cache": false
      },
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "df = pd.read_parquet(\"//Users/AstridMoeller/AEF/data/stock_characteristics_monthly.parquet\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Reading in the file\n",
        "#| df = pd.read_parquet(\"/Users/esmeraldalarsson/Desktop/Advanced Empirical Finance/stock_characteristics_monthly.parquet\")\n",
        "df.head()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Keep observations from January 2000 onward\n",
        "df[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"date\"])\n",
        "df = df.loc[df[\"date\"] >= \"2000-01-01\"].copy()"
      ],
      "id": "80322265",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We select ten stock characteristics and four macro variables capturing key GKX themes and macroeconomic conditions, including valuation, interest rates, and credit risk."
      ],
      "id": "65088afe"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "stock_chars = [\n",
        "    \"characteristic_mom12m\",  # 12-month momentum\n",
        "    \"characteristic_mom1m\",   # Short-term reversal (replaces r2_1 — not in dataset)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_mvel1\",   # Size (log market cap)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_bm\",      # Book-to-market (value)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_roaq\",    # Return on assets (profitability)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_ep\",      # Earnings-to-price\n",
        "    \"characteristic_turn\",    # Share turnover (liquidity)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_beta\",    # Market beta\n",
        "    \"characteristic_invest\",  # Investment growth\n",
        "    \"characteristic_acc\",     # Accruals\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "macro_vars = [\n",
        "    \"macro_dp\",   # Dividend-price ratio\n",
        "    \"macro_ep\",   # Earnings-price ratio\n",
        "    \"macro_tbl\",  # T-bill rate\n",
        "    \"macro_dfy\",  # Default yield spread\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "base_cols = [\"permno\", \"date\", \"ret_excess\", \"mktcap_lag\", \"sic2\"]\n",
        "df = df[base_cols + stock_chars + macro_vars].copy()"
      ],
      "id": "85adb8da",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The selected characteristics are economically interpretable and widely used in asset pricing. In particular, mom12m, mvel1, bm, and roaq capture momentum, size, value, and profitability. These are complemented by variables reflecting valuation, trading activity, risk, investment, and accounting quality. The macro variables summarize market valuation, interest rates, and credit conditions, which are standard predictors of returns.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1b: Summary Statistics and Missing Values\n",
        "Table 1 reports summary statistics for the selected predictors."
      ],
      "id": "4a10a8f3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "summary_table = (\n",
        "    df[stock_chars + macro_vars]\n",
        "    .describe()\n",
        "    .T[[\"mean\", \"std\", \"min\", \"25%\", \"50%\", \"75%\", \"max\"]]\n",
        "    .round(3)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "summary_table"
      ],
      "id": "710bbed0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The selected stock characteristics are approx centered around zero and bounded close to the [−1,1] interval, which is consistent with the scaling used in the provided dataset. The macro variables display much less cross-sectional variation because they are common state variables observed at the monthly level.\n",
        "\n",
        "To document data quality, we first count missing values before imputing."
      ],
      "id": "ef74cefe"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "missing_table = (\n",
        "    df[stock_chars + macro_vars]\n",
        "    .isna()\n",
        "    .sum()\n",
        "    .rename(\"n_missing\")\n",
        "    .to_frame()\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "missing_table"
      ],
      "id": "b8affbb6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Missing stock characteristics are imputed using the cross-sectional monthly median, preserving the cross section and using only contemporaneous information. Macroeconomic variables are forward- and backward-filled, reflecting their time-series nature. Observations with missing excess returns or lagged market capitalization are dropped, as these are required for later analysis.\n"
      ],
      "id": "7becb3f0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Impute stock characteristics with monthly cross-sectional medians\n",
        "for col in stock_chars:\n",
        "    df[col] = df.groupby(\"date\")[col].transform(lambda x: x.fillna(x.median()))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Fill macro variables over time\n",
        "df[macro_vars] = df[macro_vars].ffill().bfill()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Drop rows missing target or portfolio weight variable\n",
        "df = df.dropna(subset=[\"ret_excess\", \"mktcap_lag\"]).copy()"
      ],
      "id": "727b61f5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "post_missing = (\n",
        "    df[stock_chars + macro_vars + [\"ret_excess\", \"mktcap_lag\"]]\n",
        "    .isna()\n",
        "    .sum()\n",
        "    .rename(\"n_missing\")\n",
        "    .to_frame()\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "post_missing"
      ],
      "id": "db59697b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Because the assignment allows the use of sic2, we recode the detailed SIC groups into the 10 major SIC divisions. This keeps the dimensionality manageable while retaining broad industry information."
      ],
      "id": "5cd5d307"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def recode_sic(sic):\n",
        "    if pd.isna(sic):\n",
        "        return \"Other\"\n",
        "    sic = int(sic)\n",
        "    if sic < 10:\n",
        "        return \"Agriculture\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 15:\n",
        "        return \"Mining\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 18:\n",
        "        return \"Construction\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 40:\n",
        "        return \"Manufacturing\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 50:\n",
        "        return \"Transportation\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 52:\n",
        "        return \"Wholesale\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 60:\n",
        "        return \"Retail\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 70:\n",
        "        return \"Finance\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 90:\n",
        "        return \"Services\"\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        return \"Public Admin\"\n",
        "\n",
        "df[\"sic_division\"] = df[\"sic2\"].apply(recode_sic)"
      ],
      "id": "b8a864f7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "industry_counts = (\n",
        "    df[\"sic_division\"]\n",
        "    .value_counts()\n",
        "    .rename_axis(\"sic_division\")\n",
        "    .reset_index(name=\"n_obs\")\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "industry_counts"
      ],
      "id": "d8a55398",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As a simple illustration of the target variable, Figure 1 plots the cross-sectional mean excess return by month."
      ],
      "id": "d04f97c9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "monthly_mean_ret = (\n",
        "    df.groupby(\"date\", as_index=False)[\"ret_excess\"]\n",
        "    .mean()\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "(\n",
        "    ggplot(monthly_mean_ret, aes(x=\"date\", y=\"ret_excess\"))\n",
        "    + geom_line(size=0.4)\n",
        "    + geom_hline(yintercept=0, linetype=\"dashed\")\n",
        "    + scale_y_continuous(labels=percent_format())\n",
        "    + labs(\n",
        "        title=\"Cross-sectional mean excess return over time\",\n",
        "        x=\"\",\n",
        "        y=\"Mean excess return\"\n",
        "    )\n",
        "    + theme_minimal()\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "86816b24",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Overall, the sample remains large and suited for machine learning. The missing-value treatment preserves the panel structure and follows a conservative logic: firm-level predictors are imputed cross-sectionally within month, whereas macroeconomic predictors are completed along the time dimension.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1c: Feature matrix construction\n",
        "The assignment asks for a feature matrix consisting of stock characteristics and all interactions between stock characteristics and macroeconomic variables. With 10 stock characteristics and 4 macro predictors, this yields 10+10×4=50 continuous predictors. We then add industry dummies based on the recoded SIC divisions."
      ],
      "id": "ec4b1bce"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Create stock characteristic × macro interactions\n",
        "interaction_cols = []\n",
        "for s in stock_chars:\n",
        "    for m in macro_vars:\n",
        "        name = f\"{s}_x_{m}\"\n",
        "        df[name] = df[s] * df[m]\n",
        "        interaction_cols.append(name)\n",
        "\n",
        "# One-hot encode broad industry groups\n",
        "sic_dummies = pd.get_dummies(df[\"sic_division\"], prefix=\"sic\", drop_first=True, dtype=int)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Final feature sets\n",
        "continuous_features = stock_chars + interaction_cols\n",
        "dummy_features = list(sic_dummies.columns)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Build X, y, and metadata objects for later exercises\n",
        "X = pd.concat(\n",
        "    [df[continuous_features].reset_index(drop=True),\n",
        "     sic_dummies.reset_index(drop=True)],\n",
        "    axis=1\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "y = df[\"ret_excess\"].reset_index(drop=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "meta = df[[\"permno\", \"date\", \"mktcap_lag\", \"ret_excess\"]].reset_index(drop=True)"
      ],
      "id": "fc41650d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "feature_overview = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"object\": [\"X\", \"y\", \"meta\"],\n",
        "    \"shape\": [X.shape, y.shape, meta.shape]\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "feature_overview"
      ],
      "id": "cafecfd9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"feature_group\": [\"stock characteristics\", \"interaction terms\", \"industry dummies\", \"total features\"],\n",
        "    \"count\": [len(stock_chars), len(interaction_cols), len(dummy_features), X.shape[1]]\n",
        "})"
      ],
      "id": "bcdbbc61",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The final feature matrix contains the 10 selected stock characteristics, 40 interaction terms between stock characteristics and macroeconomic variables, and industry dummies for the broad SIC divisions. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Exercise 2\n",
        "\n",
        "### Exercise 2a: Model descriptions\n",
        "\n",
        "**Elastic Net** is a penalized linear regression model that combines the $L_1$ penalty from the Lasso and the $L_2$ penalty from Ridge regression. It minimizes the residual sum of squares subject to a penalty on coefficient size, which helps control overfitting in high-dimensional settings with correlated predictors. This is particularly relevant in our setting, as the feature matrix includes stock characteristics, interaction terms, and industry dummies. The key hyperparameters are the overall penalty strength (`alpha`) and the mixing parameter (`l1_ratio`), where `l1_ratio = 1` corresponds to the Lasso and `l1_ratio = 0` corresponds to Ridge.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Neural Network (MLP)** approximates $g(z_{i,t})$ as a composition of linear transformations and nonlinear activation functions across multiple hidden layers. Each neuron computes $x_k^l = f\\left(\\theta_0^k + \\sum_j z_j \\theta_j^k\\right)$, where $f$ is the ReLU activation function. The network is trained by minimizing the mean squared prediction error via backpropagation. The key hyperparameters are the number and size of hidden layers (`hidden_layer_sizes`), the $L_2$ regularization strength (`alpha`), and the maximum number of iterations (`max_iter`).\n",
        "\n",
        "Together, the two models provide a comparison between a structured, regularized linear benchmark and a more flexible nonlinear model.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Exercise 2b: Time-respecting data split"
      ],
      "id": "b1bf1fac"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
        "\n",
        "# Split by unique dates rather than raw rows\n",
        "unique_dates = sorted(meta[\"date\"].unique())\n",
        "\n",
        "n_dates = len(unique_dates)\n",
        "test_start = int(n_dates * 0.80)\n",
        "val_start = int(test_start * 0.75)\n",
        "\n",
        "train_dates = unique_dates[:val_start]\n",
        "val_dates   = unique_dates[val_start:test_start]\n",
        "test_dates  = unique_dates[test_start:]\n",
        "\n",
        "train_mask = meta[\"date\"].isin(train_dates)\n",
        "val_mask   = meta[\"date\"].isin(val_dates)\n",
        "test_mask  = meta[\"date\"].isin(test_dates)\n",
        "\n",
        "X_train = X.loc[train_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "X_val   = X.loc[val_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "X_test  = X.loc[test_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "\n",
        "y_train = y.loc[train_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "y_val   = y.loc[val_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "y_test  = y.loc[test_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "\n",
        "meta_train = meta.loc[train_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "meta_val   = meta.loc[val_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "meta_test  = meta.loc[test_mask].reset_index(drop=True).copy()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Standardize continuous predictors using training data only\n",
        "continuous_features = stock_chars + interaction_cols\n",
        "\n",
        "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
        "X_train[continuous_features] = scaler.fit_transform(X_train[continuous_features])\n",
        "X_val[continuous_features]   = scaler.transform(X_val[continuous_features])\n",
        "X_test[continuous_features]  = scaler.transform(X_test[continuous_features])\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Train:      {meta_train['date'].min().date()} to {meta_train['date'].max().date()} ({len(X_train):,} obs)\")\n",
        "print(f\"Validation: {meta_val['date'].min().date()} to {meta_val['date'].max().date()} ({len(X_val):,} obs)\")\n",
        "print(f\"Test:       {meta_test['date'].min().date()} to {meta_test['date'].max().date()} ({len(X_test):,} obs)\")\n",
        "print(f\"Number of train months: {len(train_dates)}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Number of validation months: {len(val_dates)}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Number of test months: {len(test_dates)}\")"
      ],
      "id": "5c2c5011",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The dataset is split chronologically to avoid look-ahead bias: all training observations precede validation observations, which precede test observations. The most recent 20% of observations form the test set. Among the remaining 80%, we allocate 75% to training and 25% to validation, yielding an approximate 60/20/20 split overall. Continuous predictors are standardized using the training data only, and the same transformation is applied to the validation and test sets to avoid data leakage.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Exercise 2c: Hyperparameter tuning"
      ],
      "id": "b80b718f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "cache": true
      },
      "source": [
        "import itertools\n",
        "from sklearn.linear_model import ElasticNet\n",
        "from sklearn.neural_network import MLPRegressor\n",
        "from sklearn.metrics import root_mean_squared_error"
      ],
      "id": "dfe0fffe",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "cache": true
      },
      "source": [
        "# ── Elastic Net tuning ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
        "# Compact grid to keep computation tractable on large panel data\n",
        "en_param_grid = {\n",
        "    \"alpha\":    [0.001, 0.1],\n",
        "    \"l1_ratio\": [0.2, 0.8],\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "en_results = []\n",
        "for params in [dict(zip(en_param_grid, v)) for v in itertools.product(*en_param_grid.values())]:\n",
        "    model = ElasticNet(max_iter=5000, fit_intercept=True, **params)\n",
        "    model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "    train_rmse = root_mean_squared_error(y_train, model.predict(X_train))\n",
        "    val_rmse   = root_mean_squared_error(y_val,   model.predict(X_val))\n",
        "    en_results.append({**params, \"train_rmse\": train_rmse, \"val_rmse\": val_rmse})\n",
        "\n",
        "en_results_df = pd.DataFrame(en_results).sort_values(\"val_rmse\")\n",
        "print(\"Elastic Net tuning results:\")\n",
        "print(en_results_df.round(6).to_string(index=False))\n",
        "\n",
        "best_en_params = en_results_df.iloc[0][list(en_param_grid.keys())].to_dict()\n",
        "print(f\"\\nBest Elastic Net params: {best_en_params}\")"
      ],
      "id": "67108b3a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "cache": true
      },
      "source": [
        "# ── Neural Network tuning ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
        "# Compact grid: 1 architecture × 2 regularization strengths = 2 fits\n",
        "nn_param_grid = {\n",
        "    \"hidden_layer_sizes\": [(32, 16)],\n",
        "    \"alpha\":              [0.001, 0.01],\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "nn_results = []\n",
        "for params in [dict(zip(nn_param_grid, v)) for v in itertools.product(*nn_param_grid.values())]:\n",
        "    model = MLPRegressor(\n",
        "        activation=\"relu\", solver=\"adam\",\n",
        "        max_iter=50, random_state=42, **params\n",
        "    )\n",
        "    model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "    train_rmse = root_mean_squared_error(y_train, model.predict(X_train))\n",
        "    val_rmse   = root_mean_squared_error(y_val,   model.predict(X_val))\n",
        "    nn_results.append({\n",
        "        \"hidden_layers\": str(params[\"hidden_layer_sizes\"]),\n",
        "        \"alpha\": params[\"alpha\"],\n",
        "        \"train_rmse\": train_rmse,\n",
        "        \"val_rmse\": val_rmse\n",
        "    })\n",
        "\n",
        "nn_results_df = pd.DataFrame(nn_results).sort_values(\"val_rmse\")\n",
        "print(\"Neural Network tuning results:\")\n",
        "print(nn_results_df.round(6).to_string(index=False))\n",
        "\n",
        "best_nn_params = nn_results_df.iloc[0]\n",
        "print(f\"\\nBest NN: layers={best_nn_params['hidden_layers']}, alpha={best_nn_params['alpha']}\")"
      ],
      "id": "706bbca8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "cache": true
      },
      "source": [
        "import ast\n",
        "\n",
        "# ── Fit final models on best hyperparameters ──────────────────────────────────\n",
        "final_en = ElasticNet(\n",
        "    alpha=best_en_params[\"alpha\"],\n",
        "    l1_ratio=best_en_params[\"l1_ratio\"],\n",
        "    max_iter=5000,\n",
        "    fit_intercept=True\n",
        ")\n",
        "final_en.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "best_hidden = ast.literal_eval(best_nn_params[\"hidden_layers\"])\n",
        "final_nn = MLPRegressor(\n",
        "    hidden_layer_sizes=best_hidden,\n",
        "    alpha=best_nn_params[\"alpha\"],\n",
        "    activation=\"relu\", solver=\"adam\",\n",
        "    max_iter=50, random_state=42\n",
        ")\n",
        "final_nn.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Final models fitted successfully.\")\n",
        "print(f\"Elastic Net    — val RMSE: {root_mean_squared_error(y_val, final_en.predict(X_val)):.6f}\")\n",
